ely to not show up for court and what  we saw is it was people who were driving  on suspended driver's licenses and so  you thought they were we were talking  about what should they were saying Oh  bail should probably be high and the  idea that it's mostly working people who  have kids who have jobs who are trying  to drive their kids to school and that  those folks are the people that pay the  consequences or face jail because  they're broke  it is is not I think what the system is  designed to do and I think you're seeing  a lot of that right now where people are  saying look the police are not acting in  a way that's consistent with trying to  uphold law it's more about  kind of the oppression of some versus  the oppression of others and and so  that's it so you you end up in jail  basically you can't pay something the  consequences increase and we look at it  like we just started a new software and  the reason we did is because we found  payments were really difficult and so we  launched a second software a second  product called promise pay and the  reason is is because we were looking at  parking tickets how do people end up in  trouble and we looked at one city and we  said how could I look if I want to put  myself on a payment plan and the  requirement was you have 200 ver like  $500 so you have to wait until it's so  expensive then you have to pay 50% up  front then you have to bring your tax  returns to prove that you deserve to put  yourself on a paint plan and you have to  go down to this office downtown during  the work week like it's just the system  isn't designed to make someone  successful and so part we thought about  is how do you build kind of products  that actually are designed for people to  succeed could you imagine if I call my  cable bill and I said I can't pay it and  they said we'll bring your tax returns  come down like this it's not reasonable  yeah and it feels like some of this is  intentional I mean some of it is just  frankly you've got bureaucratic  organizations that have no clue the  realities on the ground right so  something as simple as you know a  quote-unquote good intentioned rule is  hey bring your tax plan we'd love to get  you on a payment plan sounds like we're  helping people but for all the reasons  you're explaining actually end up  causing more and more of a problem right  yeah you know it's weird until I was in  the system I thought it was just like  racism or other folks when you're in it  you realize this is actually designed  that way and the more I minute the more  I see it  I joke that being in a system I've  become a conspiracy theorist and and for  me it was made clear when someone said  to me I said oh this product will work  this way it'll be great and the person  who runs the system said oh that's this  is a difference I don't care about  people in the system and I was like that  level of clarity of just we don't care  about people in the system was was very  important for me to understand I  remember someone saying about a prison  system that we talked to and they said  you know we wouldn't let our dogs be in  this prison  and so the idea that the people who  might be operating a system don't think  that dog their own dog should be in that  system or have no care um speaks to how  the system works and I've definitely  become more I think the system is design  is working the way it was designed to  work so explain that a little bit more  when you say design this way do you mean  design to imprison people and this is  more of like the private prison type you  know issues or is this more of just  putting as many obstacles in place as  possible and then therefore that either  leads to more revenue for the government  and possibly jail time or just clarify  that a little bit to be I think there's  a dehumanization of black and brown  people and in poor people and then I  think it there is just the reality of  the consequences I think no system that  punishes people for